WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.869
Troubleshooting llama.cpp starts by separating symptoms.

00:00:03.869 --> 00:00:09.051
Slow model loading may point to disk speed, file size, or storage location.

00:00:09.051 --> 00:00:14.578
Slow prompt processing may come from excessive context length or CPU-bound work.

00:00:14.578 --> 00:00:23.835
Slow token generation may come from model size, quantization choice, missing GPU acceleration, thermal throttling, or hardware limits.

00:00:23.835 --> 00:00:29.501
Out-of-memory errors point toward model size, context length, or offload settings.

00:00:29.501 --> 00:00:36.409
GPU offload is helpful only when the runtime, drivers, hardware, and model configuration support it.

00:00:36.409 --> 00:00:41.038
Too little VRAM may limit how much of the model can run on the GPU.

00:00:41.038 --> 00:00:44.562
Too aggressive offload can fail or become unstable.

00:00:44.562 --> 00:00:53.681
CPU inference may still be useful for smaller models or low-volume workflows, but it should be recognized as a performance tradeoff.

00:00:53.681 --> 00:00:59.139
Quantization tradeoffs can look like quality problems, speed problems, or both.

00:00:59.139 --> 00:01:03.837
A very small quantization may fit easily but produce weaker answers.

00:01:03.837 --> 00:01:08.742
A larger variant may answer better but run too slowly or exceed memory.

00:01:08.742 --> 00:01:18.346
Context length can also distort results: large context may help document workflows but increase memory pressure and prompt processing time.

00:01:18.346 --> 00:01:21.247
Measure before changing too many settings.

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:29.123
Keep a baseline prompt, record model file, context length, offload setting, sampling settings, and observed speed.

00:01:29.123 --> 00:01:31.196
Change one variable at a time.

00:01:31.196 --> 00:01:38.450
Distinguish model quality problems from runtime, prompt formatting, hardware, and configuration problems.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:44.115
A disciplined troubleshooting loop saves time and creates knowledge you can reuse.
